<header><p class="eyebrow">November 13, 2026 UTC · Men and women · Authenticated owner result</p><h1>The run stopped<br>before science.</h1><p class="intro">B063R6 ended at its first shared resource check after 1.749 seconds. B063R7 adds bounded fresh checks and permanent live execution logs, while retaining the completed work and every dated scientific report.</p></header>
<div class="cards" id="r7-cards"><div class="card"><strong>1.749s</strong><span>Actual R6 worker time<br>before the admission stop</span></div><div class="card"><strong>0</strong><span>New scientific fits<br>in the actual R6 attempt</span></div><div class="card"><strong>22</strong><span>Prior target fits preserved<br>134 saved objects authenticated</span></div><div class="card"><strong>R7</strong><span>Replacement prepared<br>owner execution pending</span></div></div>
<aside class="notice" id="r7-scope"><p><strong>The latest failure is admission, with no new scientific work.</strong> The owner return contains no startup-test or native-preflight result because neither stage was entered. The earlier deadlock repair and successful scoped recovery remain part of the authenticated history.</p><p>R6 rechecked the two exact old process identities as dead, on a different boot. It sent no new signals and needed no new process-control action. The <a href="b063r6.html">dated R5 recovery and R6 preparation report</a> remains unchanged.</p></aside>
<section id="r7-failure"><h2>What the actual return proves</h2><div class="grid"><article class="panel"><h3>The first shared lease was denied</h3><p>The snapshot had <strong>48 effective vCPUs</strong>, 95,348,129,792 available memory bytes and 33,972,879,360 free disk bytes. Its IO full-pressure average over ten seconds was <strong>20.14%</strong>. The shared coordinator reserved <strong>zero CPUs</strong>.</p><p>The CPU counter needed another sample before current spare capacity could be measured. An earlier capacity estimate did not create a shared reservation. The worker ended without a fresh shared check.</p></article><article class="panel"><h3>The short return preserved evidence</h3><p>The final return was written, persisted and read back. All <strong>22 prior target-fit receipts and 134 exact objects</strong> remain authenticated. R6 added zero fits, checkpoints, forecasts, market quotes and scores.</p><p class="muted">The return contains one independent observer sample. One sample cannot prove a ten-second interval or twelve-core scientific utilization.</p></article></div><details id="r7-clocks"><summary>Inspect timing, cache and cost limits</summary><p>The actual worker started at <time datetime="2026-11-13T16:18:28.499178+00:00">16:18:28.499 UTC</time> and recorded completion at <time datetime="2026-11-13T16:18:29.845076+00:00">16:18:29.845 UTC on November 13</time>. The worker timer reports 1.748999 seconds; its admission timer reports 1.006134 seconds. These scopes are not added.</p><p>Pre-science status reports zero full historical S3 GETs and zero local byte-cache reuses. Historical-cache consumption never began, so this is not a successful cache-rerun benchmark. Return persistence is a separate transfer.</p><p>No current instance rate was verified in this attempt. Dollar cost remains unavailable; an old rate is not silently reused.</p></details></section>
<section id="r7-repair"><h2>The executable changes</h2><div class="grid"><article class="panel"><h3>Wait for fresh evidence within the existing budget</h3><p>R7 retries the atomic shared coordinator within the original admission budget, capped at 120 seconds. It retains the resource guards, one-second polling, cooperative stop, explicit failure receipts and ten-second progress reporting.</p><p>It never starts a fit without a valid reservation, overrides IO pressure or signals another owner. A continuing denial returns preserved work with the measured reason; resource recovery has no invented ETA.</p></article><article class="panel"><h3>Observe the run outside the workload</h3><p>Each prepared NCAA invocation writes separate <code>LIVE_EXECUTION.log</code> and <code>LIVE_EXECUTION.jsonl</code> streams with a default ten-second cadence. The independent supervisor captures output, phase, measured CPU, child identities, memory and current bound workload counters.</p><p>Fit counts, cache reuse, S3 activity, cost, ETA and checkpoint status stay unavailable until supported by the bound current workload. A busy process never becomes an assumed completed fit.</p></article></div><details id="r7-engineering"><summary>Inspect final verification and the prepared fingerprint</summary><p id="r7-tests">__B063R7_TESTS__</p><p>Prepared source SHA-256: <code id="r7-source">__B063R7_SOURCE__</code>.</p><p>Preparation receives zero scientific-fit credit. R7 owner execution and measured scientific throughput remain pending.</p></details></section>
<section id="r7-logging"><h2>All four project selectors were exercised</h2><p>The shared logger was recovered from the current repository and reused exactly. The new explicit-command supervisor ran four real local children concurrently, one for each project. Each child waited while both live files grew; this is a failure-injection check, not a scientific CPU benchmark.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="scope-table" id="r7-logging-table"><thead><tr><th>Project</th><th>Measured maximum gap</th><th>Local logging result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>NFL</td><td>10.006012 s</td><td>Pass</td></tr><tr><td>FBS</td><td>10.006147 s</td><td>Pass</td></tr><tr><td>NBA</td><td>10.005574 s</td><td>Pass</td></tr><tr><td>NCAA basketball</td><td>10.006067 s</td><td>Pass</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Nine focused tests also verify failed commands, child signal exits, scoped signal forwarding, bound progress and preservation of previous log bytes. No logging test receives sports-fit credit.</p><aside class="notice" id="r7-activation"><p><strong>Tested interface; private production activation remains separate.</strong> NCAA R7 integrates the logger in its prepared executable. Current repository evidence already records use of the shared framework in the prepared NBA B082 replacement. The latest main also adds an NFL N085 logging adapter built on the same unchanged logger; that adapter and its checks are retained. NFL and FBS private executable bodies remain unavailable in the public files.</p><p>The generic supervisor can wrap an explicitly supplied existing command for each sport. This work does not claim every deployed private runner was modified, installed or owner executed. <a href="https://github.com/alvaromendizabal/sports-edge-lab/tree/main/runtime/execution">Inspect the shared source, activation contract and tests.</a></p></aside></section>
<section id="r7-remaining"><h2>The scientific frontier stays open</h2><p>The dated comparison retains 164 historical candidate panels, 28 per-season leaders, 14 pooled metric roles and 14 current observed replay leaders. Original issue clocks, frozen evaluation populations, negative findings and provider gaps remain unchanged. This repair does not declare a new champion.</p><ol><li>Resume compatible saved fits, checkpoints and cached inputs after the repaired gates pass.</li><li>Demonstrate actual simultaneous scientific fits, CPU use and throughput on the safely available twelve-core project allocation.</li><li>Refresh every eligible current-season and upcoming game, with outcomes and valid pregame issue clocks.</li><li>Reevaluate former, current and rolling champions on the required seasons and seven metrics, with genuine time-matched DraftKings, Kalshi and Polymarket quotes where available.</li></ol><div class="links"><a class="primary" href="index.html">Open preserved NCAA dashboard</a><a href="b063r6.html">Inspect the dated recovery</a><a id="r7-download" href="data/B063R7_PUBLIC_STATUS.json" download>Download exact status JSON</a></div></section>
<footer id="r7-publication">Sanitized operational evidence. Source publication is separate from website deployment. No new scores, quotes, forecasts or complete current-season coverage are claimed. The exact observations and engineering boundaries are in the downloadable JSON.</footer>
